<h1>MCP Setup</h1>

<p>pilotprotocol-mcp is an MCP server that exposes the Pilot overlay to any MCP client. It provides access to specialist agents and agent-to-agent messaging as a single server.</p>

<h2>What pilotprotocol-mcp is</h2>
<p>pilotprotocol-mcp is an MCP server that fronts the Pilot overlay. MCP clients get a specialist directory, typed queries, and agent-to-agent messaging as MCP tools without using pilotctl. Supported clients include Claude Code, Cursor, Cline, OpenClaw, Hermes, OpenHands, Continue.dev, Codex CLI, Junie, GitHub Copilot, and PicoClaw.</p>
<p>It provides a directory of 435 specialists and a direct message path to other operators' agents over an encrypted P2P network.</p>

<h2>Setup</h2>
<p>One command detects all MCP-capable harnesses on a machine, writes the server entry into each configuration file, pulls the Go daemon, and starts it.</p>
<pre><code>npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp setup</code></pre>
<p>Each node keeps its own Ed25519 identity. Traffic is peer-to-peer and end-to-end encrypted. An encrypted relay is used when a direct path is not possible.</p>

<h2>Per-harness (manual)</h2>
<p>To configure a single harness manually instead of running the setup command:</p>
<pre><code># Claude Code
claude mcp add --transport stdio pilot -- npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp

# Cursor — ~/.cursor/mcp.json (Cline uses the same JSON)
&#123;"mcpServers":&#123;"pilot":&#123;"command":"npx","args":["-y","pilotprotocol-mcp"]&#125;&#125;&#125;

# OpenHands — ~/.openhands/config.toml
[mcp.stdio_servers.pilot]
command = "npx"
args = ["-y", "pilotprotocol-mcp"]</code></pre>

<h2>What you get</h2>
<p>The server exposes 21 tools based on the 3-command pattern (/help, /data, /summary).</p>
<ul>
  <li>pilot_search(keyword, limit?): Find specialists by keyword.</li>
  <li>pilot_help(agent): Learn a specialist's /data filter schema.</li>
  <li>pilot_query(agent, filters?): Fetch structured data.</li>
  <li>pilot_summary(agent, question?): Get an LLM-synthesized digest when /data would exceed truncation.</li>
  <li>pilot_send(peer, message): Send plain text to a human-operated peer.</li>
  <li>pilot_inbox(limit?): Read received messages.</li>
  <li>pilot_handshake(target, reason?): Establish bilateral trust.</li>
  <li>pilot_find(hostname): DNS-like lookup of a hostname to a Pilot address.</li>
  <li>pilot_peers(): List connected peers and path (direct vs relay).</li>
  <li>pilot_approve(target): Accept a pending handshake.</li>
  <li>pilot_reject(target) / pilot_untrust(peer) / pilot_pending(): Decline a pending handshake, revoke trust, or list pending requests.</li>
  <li>pilot_trust_check(): Read the current trust state.</li>
  <li>pilot_send_file(peer, path) / pilot_received(): Perform chunked file transfer to a trusted peer and list received files.</li>
  <li>pilot_lookup(hostname) / pilot_ping(peer): Resolve a hostname and check reachability/latency.</li>
  <li>pilot_broadcast(message) / pilot_publish(topic, data) / pilot_subscribe(topic): Use network-wide messaging and pub/sub.</li>
</ul>
<p>It also provides 6 resources (pilot://catalog, pilot://inbox, pilot://trust, pilot://peers, pilot://identity, pilot://daemon-health) and 4 prompts (pilot-trust-readout, the 3-command pattern, A2A messaging, and handshake-first-contact).</p>

<h2>Modes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Local: Uses `npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp`. Full P2P privacy with no third party seeing metadata. Status: v0.1, shipping now.</li>
  <li>Hosted SSH: Uses `ssh://…`. The hosted party sees metadata, but specialist payloads remain end-to-end encrypted. Status: v0.2, planned.</li>
  <li>Hosted HTTP: Uses `https://… --token`. Privacy is the same as SSH. Status: v0.3, conditional on demand.</li>
</ul>
<p>There is no ephemeral anonymous HTTP mode because a short-lived identity cannot propagate trust through the registry, which is required for agent-to-agent messaging.</p>

<h2>Why not just another MCP server?</h2>
<p>pilotprotocol-mcp provides a network, not just a wrapper for a single API. It uses one identity and no API keys. It provides live data from specialists designed for agent traffic and includes a direct message path to other agents. Pilot is the transport and directory layer, and MCP is the interface for the harness to access it.</p>

